WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.120
How long does a known hole
stay open on our systems?

00:00:03.515 --> 00:00:05.285
Patching fails in four ways.

00:00:06.617 --> 00:00:08.307
First, the fix is delayed.

00:00:08.692 --> 00:00:10.202
When a vendor publishes a fix,

00:00:10.832 --> 00:00:12.662
scanners sweep the internet within hours.

00:00:13.062 --> 00:00:16.952
Ours sits in a queue, three weeks
from the next maintenance window.

00:00:17.222 --> 00:00:21.772
The number that matters is the days
between fix published and fix applied.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.080
Second, software that cannot be patched.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.860
The vendor stopped support years ago,
but it still runs payroll.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.400
Every new hole stays open for good.

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:35.820
Windows 10 support ended
on 14 October 2025.

00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:39.667
Third, the box at the
edge that nobody owns.

00:00:40.547 --> 00:00:43.457
A VPN or firewall appliance
faces the internet.

00:00:43.787 --> 00:00:47.677
The vendor's critical notice
goes to a mailbox nobody reads.

00:00:48.037 --> 00:00:52.087
One appliance with no owner
undoes every control behind it.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.440
Fourth, systems we did not know we had.

00:00:55.675 --> 00:00:59.845
A test server, an agency website,
a cloud machine from a trial.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.820
None is on the inventory,
so none is patched.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.860
Attackers scan everything
with our name on it.

00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:07.993
What good looks like.

00:01:08.278 --> 00:01:11.268
A complete inventory,
kept current automatically.

00:01:11.708 --> 00:01:15.298
Patch deadlines, in days for
anything the internet can reach.

00:01:15.623 --> 00:01:18.513
An emergency path outside
the change window.

00:01:18.773 --> 00:01:20.963
A named owner for every edge device.

00:01:21.383 --> 00:01:25.693
NIS2 names vulnerability handling
as a risk-management measure.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:28.640
A suggested first step.

00:01:28.925 --> 00:01:33.815
Ask how many days a critical fix for
an internet-facing system waits today.

00:01:34.145 --> 00:01:38.035
Then ask whether that number is on
a dashboard reported to the board.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.730
If nobody can answer the first,
the second is the problem.

00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:44.913
Known hole, published fix.

00:01:45.318 --> 00:01:47.328
Measure the days, and name an owner.

